Encapsulation is the process of wrapping data (variables) and methods (functions) into a single unit (class) while restricting direct access to the data.

Making variables private
Providing public getter and setter methods to access and modify those variables

Why is Encapsulation Used?

Encapsulation provides several benefits:

1. Data Security

No one can directly modify important data.
2. Better Control

You decide:

Who can read the data
Who can change the data
What values are acceptable
3. Easy Maintenance

If the internal implementation changes, other classes do not need to change.

4. Improved Security

Sensitive information like:

Passwords
Bank balances
Medical records
Employee salaries

cannot be directly accessed.


What is a Setter?

A setter is a method that sets (updates) the value of a private variable.

public void setName(String name) {
    this.name = name;
}

When you call:

s.setName("John");

the value "John" is stored in the private variable name.

What is a Getter?

A getter is a method that returns (reads) the value of a private variable.

public String getName() {
    return name;
}

When you call:

System.out.println(s.getName());

it prints:

John


