Constructor
A constructor in Java is a special block of code used to initialize newly created objects. 
A constructor is a special method in Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) that is automatically called when an object of a class is created.

It is mainly used to initialize the object (give initial values to variables, set up resources, etc.).
What is a Constructor?

A constructor is a function inside a class that:

Has the same name as the class (in many languages like Java, C++)
Runs automatically when an object is created
Does not return any value (not even void in some languages)
Why we use Constructors in Java?

We use constructors to:

Initialize variables when object is created
Avoid writing extra setup code
Make objects ready to use immediately
Types of Constructors in Java
1. Default Constructor


No parameters

class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    // Default Constructor
    Student() {
        name = "Unknown";
        age = 0;
    }

    // Method to display values
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Age: " + age);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Object creation → constructor automatically called
        Student s1 = new Student();

        // calling method
        s1.display();
    }
}
🔷 Output:
Name: Unknown
Age: 0
